  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Find the **polar coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{17}{4}$ intersects the negative $x$-axis. Express the polar angle in radians.\n\nThe point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "The polar curve $r=\\frac{17}{4}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{17}{4}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $x$-axis where $r=\\frac{17}{4}$ and $\\theta =\\pi .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095706/assets/652a619b125a7d26cf7cb53b254ac18858204a8972efe2d0383fbd4a81d66b0c.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(\\,\\frac{17}{4},\\pi ).$",
